1: # Exact complementary III* and IV* cap constructions
2: 
3: Status: Proved as P3F.41. The source, exact line diagrams, affine norms
4: and complete integral gluing maps passed the independent root audit.
5: The local toric correspondence also passed the independent checks audit.
6: The signed actual returns of both norm signs are proved in P3K.26.
7: P3F.37 and P3K.24 retain the earlier reduced-III construction and proof.
8: 
9: ## SC.1. Explicit sources and the parameter domains
10: 
11: The following source models retain their actual marked section:
12: 
13: | Source | Equation and G | Fibres |
14: |---|---|---|
15: | E7-A | y²=x³+tx+1, G=(0,1) | III* at infinity, three I1 |
16: | E7-B | y²=x³+tx−t, G=(1,1) | III* at infinity, II at0, I1 at−27/4 |
17: | E6-A | y²=x³−3x+t²+2, G=(1,t) | IV* at infinity, I2 at0, I1 at±2i |
18: | E6-B | y²=x³+tx+t², G=(0,t) | IV* at infinity, III at0, I1 at−4/27 |
19: 
20: The first two are verified in P3F.37/P3K.24. For E6-A the discriminant
21: is 27t²(t²+4). The factorization
22: (y−t)(y+t)=(x−1)²(x+2) gives a rational chart
23: u=(y−t)/(x−1), v=(y+t)/(x−1), with inverse
24: x=uv−2, t=(x−1)(v−u)/2, y=(x−1)(u+v)/2.
25: For E6-B its discriminant is t³(4+27t). Put
26: u=(y−t)/x, v=(y+t)/x; the inverse is
27: x=uv−(v−u)/2, t=x(v−u)/2, y=x(u+v)/2.
28: Thus these are rational elliptic surfaces, with the stated minimal
29: coefficient orders. At infinity their cubic good roots send G to a
30: nonzero fixed three-torsion point. At the I2 in E6-A, the two local
31: branch orders of G are one and one; at the III in E6-B the fourth
32: good root sends G to the nonzero fixed two-torsion point. Thus G has
33: nonidentity components in E6 and A1, height 1/6, and G.O=0.
34: The root discriminant and height identity show that G is primitive
35: and the Mordell--Weil group is torsion free.
36: 
37: Set M=O(aG−O), Q=bG. Use the following actual data, with a single
38: cap at infinity, no other logarithmic sites, and one chosen I1 as
39: the simple whole-fibre zero:
40: 
41: | Sources | (a,b) | Root order m | Root type |
42: |---|---|---|---|
43: | E7-A,B | (2,1) | m=4l+3, l≥0 | III |
44: | E7-A,B | (1,2) | m=4l, l≥1 | smooth |
45: | E6-A,B | (6,1) | m=3l+2, l≥0 | IV |
46: | E6-A,B | (2,3) | m=3l, l≥1 | smooth |
47: 
48: For each entry choose an integer n with gcd(m,n)=1. The actual cap
49: norm will be A_r^m=B_lambda^n, lambda=r^m, with marked meridian
50: m q+n gamma_out. In these domains n is nonzero. Every retained
51: reducible fibre has translation transitive on its components:
52: at the retained A1 it is odd b, while a is even. The source Hom
53: line has whole-fibre degree ab/d=1 (d=2 or6). Its degree follows
54: also directly from the section intersections: for E7 it is1;
55: for E6 (6,1), (6G).O=(5G).O=2 and G.O=0; for (2,3),
56: (2G).O=(-G).O=(-3G).O=0. At each component either M has zero
57: multidegree or Q fixes the component, so the vertical Hom divisor
58: is a whole fibre. The selected section of that base line supplies
59: the prescribed one simple I1 zero.
60: 
61: ## SC.2. Root equations and exact affine source norms
62: 
63: For E7 reduced roots use (F37.1) and the equation in K24 for E7-B.
64: For E6-A at infinity,
65: \[
66:  y_0^2=x_0^3-3q^4x_0+q^4+2q^6,\qquad G=(q^2,q^2).
67: \]
68: For E6-B,
69: \[
70:  y_0^2=x_0^3+q^3x_0+q^4,\qquad G=(0,q^2).
71: \]
72: For m=3l+2, k=2l+1, substitute q=z^m,
73: x0=z^{2k}X, y0=z^{3k}Y. The respective equations are
74: \[
75: \begin{array}{ll}
76: Y^2=X^3-3z^{4l+4}X+z^2+2z^{6l+6},&
77: G'=(z^{2l+2},z),\\
78: Y^2=X^3+z^{l+2}X+z^2,&G'=(0,z).
79: \end{array}
80: \]
81: They have the reduced IV minimal regular fibre. In the chart
82: X=zX1,Y=zY1 its two nonidentity component labels are distinguished
83: by Y1=±1. The actual deck
84: \[
85:  D(X,Y,z)=(\zeta_m^{-2k}X,\zeta_m^{-3k}Y,\zeta_mz)
86: \]
87: has character zeta_m^{-(3k+1)}=zeta_m^{-2m}=1 on Y1.
88: It fixes the three component labels. G' is nonidentity and 6G'
89: has identity component. Resolving the displayed A2 surface point
90: retains these labels and the source translation.
91: 
92: For good roots, take q=z^m with k=3m/4 for E7 and k=2m/3 for E6.
93: The central smooth curve and its deck have order four or three
94: respectively. In a coordinate w at any marked fixed section,
95: the minimal good-root chart has weights (r−1,1), r=4 or3.
96: The good-root limit of Q is O, since b is the component-group
97: order (two or three). The limit of aG is a nonzero fixed point
98: of that order. These are the exact marked limits from the source
99: coordinates, not unmarked smooth elliptic replacements.
100: 
101: There is a unique analytic m-division R of Q' with the selected
102: central point. At a reduced root multiplication by m is invertible
103: on its additive identity group and on its component group, since
104: m is prime to two or three. At a good root use the local inverse
105: of multiplication by m at O. Deck invariance follows from
106: uniqueness. The actual source map
107: \[
108:                          a_0=t_{-nR}D
109: \]
110: satisfies a0^m=t_{-nQ'}. Translation extends to the actual minimal
111: regular source by the graph/minimality argument used in F37.
112: 
113: ## SC.3. The good-root line map as a toric correspondence
114: 
115: Let r=3 or4 be the minimal good root. Near a marked root section
116: choose its holomorphic coordinate w=0. Centering at the actual
117: equivariant section preserves the diagonal weights (r−1,1).
118: The quotient lattice is
119: \[
120:  N'=\mathbb Z^2+\mathbb Z(r-1,1)/r.
121: \]
122: The minimal quotient resolution has rays
123: \[
124:  v_j=((r-j)/r,j/r),\qquad 0\le j\le r.
125: \]
126: Consecutive cones have determinant 1/r in the ordinary lattice,
127: hence are regular in N'. The following actual common refinements
128: are regular in the original root lattice:
129: \[
130: \begin{array}{ll}
131: r=3:&(1,0),(2,1),(1,1),(1,2),(0,1),\\
132: r=4:&(1,0),(3,1),(2,1),(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(0,1).
133: \end{array}
134: \]
135: Every adjacent determinant is one. Let beta be the resulting root
136: blowup and pi its toric map to the quotient minimal resolution.
137: The underlying lattice inclusion gives every chart map by its
138: dual monomials. Thus pi is an actual proper generically finite
139: map; no finiteness of its exceptional fibres is assumed.
140: 
141: The resolved strict section is the divisor of the w-axis ray.
142: Its integral Cartier support function is
143: \[
144:  h_w(v)=\max(v_w-(r-1)v_z,0).
145: \]
146: Indeed it is w−(r−1)z on the first minimal cone and zero on the
147: remaining cones. The character w−(r−1)z belongs to the quotient
148: dual lattice. Pulling that Cartier function to the common
149: refinement, while beta pulls the original section with valuation
150: v_w, gives the exact exceptional correction
151: \[
152:  \beta^*(\hbox{root section})-\pi^*(\hbox{resolved section})
153:  =\sum_{\text{exceptional }v}\min(v_w,(r-1)v_z)E_v.       \tag{SC.1}
154: \]
155: For r=3 the coefficients along the listed exceptional rays are
156: (2,1,1); for r=4 they are (3,2,1,1,1). Subtract the identical
157: O-chart contribution from the aG-chart contribution. This gives
158: an exact isomorphism
159: \[
160:  \beta^*N=\pi^*M+D_{aG}-D_O,\qquad N=O(aG'-O),           \tag{SC.2}
161: \]
162: including each exceptional coefficient.
163: 
164: At r=3 these are the three A2 quotient points of the IV* model.
165: At r=4 the two marked fixed points have A3 quotients; the other
166: two points form an order-two orbit and give the remaining A1
167: arm. No section divisor in (SC.2) passes through that latter
168: orbit. The minimal resolved quotients are the original IV* and
169: III* source models: the invariant Weierstrass substitutions give
170: their birational maps, and their crepant resolution graphs are
171: the stated affine E6 and E7 fibres, with no vertical (-1)-curve.
172: The relative minimal-model graph comparison gives the isomorphism.
173: 
174: The correction is equivariant for the actual suspension. At a
175: fixed root point, Q'_0=O and deck equivariance give
176: \[
177:  w\longmapsto w\,u(w,z)+z^{r-1}h(z^r),\qquad z\longmapsto z,
178:  \qquad u(0,0)=1.
179: \]
180: This preserves each ideal (w,z^j) and (z,w^j), 1≤j≤r−1.
181: The normalized blowup of their invariant product gives exactly
182: the displayed simultaneous fan refinement. Therefore the actual
183: source translation lifts, preserves every exceptional correction
184: in (SC.2), and gives an extending line map on N commuting with D.
185: For m=r l, pull this whole correspondence through the additional
186: base change and resolve the fibre product. The same invariant
187: ideals, now (w,z^{jl}), retain the lifted source map and the
188: pullback divisor identity. This supplies the actual line and B0,
189: not only their generic-fibre Picard classes.
190: 
191: The natural deck weights of N at the marked nonzero point and O
192: are respectively zeta_r^{r−1} and zeta_r^{-(r−1)}, up to a common
193: character. They are the weights of (SC.1) at the section. The
194: central Picard class is nonzero of order p=3 for E6 and p=2 for
195: E7. The exact connecting map
196: \[
197:  (\mathbb C/\Lambda)^D\longrightarrow\Lambda/(D-1)\Lambda,
198:  \qquad[v]\longmapsto[(D-1)v]
199: \]
200: identifies it with the primitive p-torsion class of the cap circle
201: line. A common deck character tensors by a flat line from the
202: free suspension circle and does not remove this torsion.
203: 
204: At a reduced root the source line is already zero on all original
205: components. Pullback to the resolved fibre product therefore has
206: degree zero on every contracted curve and descends through the
207: actual contractions. Its comparison with N has a whole-fibre
208: divisor, exactly as in F37, using the reduced III or IV
209: intersection matrix. This is the complementary actual line map.
210: 
211: ## SC.4. Commuting line norms, freeness and holomorphic parameter closing
212: 
213: The full lift argument is the one verified in IV.3. Lift t_{−R}
214: first by F, correcting its meromorphic chord section's vertical
215: divisor by a whole-fibre power. Average by the finite deck character
216: to arrange DFD^{-1}=chi F, chi^m=1. Since mR=Q', write the actual
217: descended map as B0=beta(z)F^m. It commutes with D by (SC.2);
218: therefore beta is a nonzero function of q. In particular F and D
219: both commute with B0. The map F^nD has norm ratio
220: \[
221:  (F^nD)^m/B_0^n=\chi^{nm(m-1)/2}\beta(q)^{-n}.
222: \]
223: An invariant analytic m-th root of this unit gives A0 with
224: A0^m=B0^n and [A0,B0]=1. The character factor is retained also
225: for the even good-root orders.
226: 
227: Put A_r=r^nA0, B_lambda=r^mB0. Choose u,v with nu+mv=1.
228: Then
229: \[
230:  H=A_r^uB_\lambda^v=rH_0,\qquad H^m=B_\lambda,\quad H^n=A_r.
231: \]
232: On a smaller compact source tube H0 has bounded norm ratios, so
233: H is strictly contracting for small |r|. The cap is exactly
234: N^times/<H>. Its norm shell proves smoothness and properness,
235: and its strict contraction proves freeness of the delta circle.
236: For outward h=H^{-1}, the single-A cut gives
237: \[
238:                          q\mapsto-nh,\qquad\gamma\mapsto mh,
239: \]
240: hence precisely m q+n gamma. All source translations and line
241: characters are retained in this cap and its annular comparison.
242: 
243: The holomorphic ordinary-to-cap translation cochain is solved
244: once at c0 and then changed by n(c−c0)s_infty delta/m.
245: Its positive cut is the actual A_r. At c->c+m its change is
246: n s_infty delta, so the inverse closing is multiplication by
247: (t−tc)^{-n}. The positive deck is its inverse. Near the selected
248: cusp this multiplier and its inverse are B_lambda iterates
249: composed with bounded source translations and invertible units.
250: They extend through its specified Mumford charts. At the other
251: fillings they are units. This constructs the proper holomorphic
252: family over the punctured r-disc, with lambda=r^m, including
253: negative n. No source root alone is used to choose a parameter
254: return class.
255: 
256: ## SC.5. Integral source and boundary maps
257: 
258: All maps below are executed with full Smith transformations in
259: code/part3/iii_star_good_complement.py and
260: code/part3/iv_star_complement.py. Their helpers are retained in
261: integer_chain.py. The E7 integral basis is O,F,E7,G as in F37.
262: For E6 use O,F,C0,...,C5,A1,G. The E6 graph has edges
263: (0,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,4),(2,5), highest-root marks
264: (1,2,3,2,1,2), and G meets C0 and the A1 component.
265: The full Gram determinant is -1. Translation by G cyclically
266: permutes the three affine E6 arms and swaps the two A1 components.
267: Its section columns come from the exact height and component
268: formula. The resulting matrix is checked to preserve the Gram
269: form, and the actual contracting source map is its negative b-th
270: power. No separate unmarked lattice is substituted.
271: 
272: Let V be the source minus its starred fibre tube. That tube has
273: k=8 or7 component classes and no odd homology. Its inclusion
274: matrix L is the affine component matrix, retaining the multiplicity
275: vector. The exact complement chain model has
276: \[
277:  C_4=\mathbb Z,\ C_3=\mathbb Z,\ C_2=\mathbb Z^{10},\
278:  C_1=\mathbb Z^k,\ C_0=\mathbb Z,\qquad
279:  d_4=1,\ d_2=L^tB.
280: \]
